(1. SETTING \(ORIENTING\) A MAP: A map is set when its top north corresponds) Tj
T*
(with the actual North on the ground. A map can be set by Compass \(aligning) Tj
T*
(magnetic line with needle\) or by Ground Features \(matching linear features) Tj
T*
(such as roads or railway lines\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2. DAY NAVIGATION & PACE COUNTING: Movement in daylight utilizes pace) Tj
T*
(counting as a dead-reckoning distance measure. For an average cadet, 120) Tj
T*
(natural double paces equals approximately 100 meters on flat terrain. In) Tj
T*
(hilly or swampy ground, pace count increases by 10% to 20%.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3. NIGHT MARCH CHARTS & LUMINOUS MARKS: Night navigation is conducted) Tj
T*
(without visible ground landmarks. Troops prepare a Night March Chart) Tj
T*
(detailing sequential legs, grid bearings, distances in paces, and) Tj
T*
(identifiable luminous waypoint objects.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(4. STEERING BY STARS: In the Northern Hemisphere, the Pole Star \(Polaris\)) Tj
T*
(identified using the Great Bear \(Ursa Major\) constellation provides a) Tj
T*
(dependable reference for True North.) Tj
